WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.889
A musician wakes from a terrible nightmare.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:11.490
In her dream, she finds herself in a society
where music education has been made mandatory.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:17.780
Since musicians are known to set their ideas
down in the form of sheet music, it is imperative

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.700
that students become fluent in this language.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:26.740
It would be ludicrous to expect a child to
sing a song or play an instrument without

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:32.829
having a thorough grounding in music notation
and theory, playing and listening to music,

00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:39.250
let alone composing an original piece are
considered very advanced topics, and are generally

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.130
put off until college, and more often graduate
school.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:50.630
Of course, not many students actually go on
to concentrate in music, so only a few will

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:55.080
ever get to hear the sounds that the black
dots represent.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.850
Most students just aren't very good at music.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.800
They are bored in class.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.090
Their skills are terrible, and their homework
is barely legible.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:09.219
Most of them couldn't care less about how
important music is in today's world.

00:01:09.219 --> 00:01:14.209
They just want to take the minimum number
of music courses and be done with it.

00:01:14.209 --> 00:01:19.420
I guess there are just music people and non
music people.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:26.490
Meanwhile, on the other side of town, a painter
has just awakened from a similar nightmare.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:31.399
She was in a regular classroom, no easels
or tubes of paint.

00:01:31.399 --> 00:01:37.209
Since the students don't apply paint until
high school, they first need to study colours

00:01:37.209 --> 00:01:39.259
and applicators.

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:45.899
In a few years, they'll be ready to take pre-paint
by numbers, which prepares them for the main

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:49.560
paint by numbers sequence in high school.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.789
The teacher said that the really excellent
painters, the ones who know their colours

00:01:54.789 --> 00:02:00.100
and brushes off by heart, get to the actual
painting a little sooner.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.609
And some of them even take the advanced placement
classes for college credit.

00:02:04.609 --> 00:02:10.860
But mostly, teachers are just trying to give
the kids a good foundation in what painting

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.350
is all about.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:17.230
So when they get out there in the real world,
and paint their kitchen, they don't make a

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.110
total mess of it.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.000
This setup is from the introduction to an
essay called Lockhart's Lament, where the

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.220
author Paul Lockhart argues that our current
math education is exactly this kind of nightmare.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.170
The essay has the ability to blow your mind
and change the way you think about learning

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.170
math.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.690
So I'd like to share some of the ideas from
it with you.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.200
There's a link to the full essay in the description.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.780
Lockhart was a successful research mathematician,
before he decided to devote his time to teaching

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:53.770
school kids, and now teaches at a school in
New York.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:59.780
He claims that when students say that math
class is stupid and boring, that they are

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.780
right.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.770
He says that the first thing to understand
is that mathematics is an art.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:11.110
And the difference between math and other
arts, like music or painting is that our culture

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.730
doesn't recognise it as such.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.070
Mathematicians enjoy thinking about the simplest
possible things.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:21.080
And the simplest possible things are imaginary.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.400
It gives an example of a triangle inside a
rectangular box.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:32.020
But he isn't talking about a drawing of the
box or of any real physical object, just the

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.830
imaginary concept.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.040
In math things of the way you want them to
be.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.190
You have endless choices, and there is no
reality to get in your way.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:48.760
You can then play with your imagination, and
wonder how much of the box does the triangle

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.940
take up?

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.980
You might find something simple and pretty.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:04:00.030
Such as if I chopped the rectangle into two
pieces like this, I can see that each piece

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.680
is cut diagonally in half by the sides of
the triangle.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.459
So there is just as much space inside the
triangle as outside.

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:13.210
And that means the triangle must take up exactly
half the box.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.590
Now, where did this idea come from?

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:19.680
How does one know to draw that line?

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.779
Well how does a painter know where to put
his brush?

00:04:22.779 --> 00:04:30.080
Inspiration, experience, trial and error and
luck, that's the art of it, creating these

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.289
beautiful little poems of thought, these sonnets
of pure reason.

00:04:35.289 --> 00:04:42.969
In school, instead of a natural question about
shapes and a creative and rewarding process

00:04:42.969 --> 00:04:50.460
of invention and discovery, students are treated
to: area is equal to half base times height,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.349
a fact to be memorised.

00:04:53.349 --> 00:04:59.370
The question has been asked and answered at
the same time and there is nothing left for

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.889
the student to do.

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:08.039
Lockhart says that if you deny students the
opportunity to engage in this activity, to

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:15.229
pose their own problems, make their own conjectures
and discoveries, to be wrong, to be creatively

00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:21.050
frustrated, to have an inspiration and to
cobble together their own explanations and

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.050
proof.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.020
Well, you deny them mathematics itself.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:31.689
Those who have become adept at it derive a
great deal of self esteem from this success.

00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:39.210
The last thing they want to hear is that math
is really about raw creativity and aesthetic

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.530
sensitivity.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:45.990
Many a graduate student has come to grief,
when they discover, after a decade of being

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:52.041
told they were good at math, that, in fact,
they have no real mathematical talent, and

00:05:52.041 --> 00:05:54.789
are just very good at following directions.

00:05:54.789 --> 00:05:59.150
I felt a bit called out by this paragraph.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:05.620
And here even after completing a university
major in math, I was left wondering if I have

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.789
even really encountered real math at all.

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:15.979
And I wondered if I was good at math just
because I was quite good at memorising things.

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:21.960
Lockhart says that this cultural problem is
a self perpetuating monster.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:27.310
Students learn about math from their teachers,
and teachers learn about it from their teachers.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:33.360
And people actually think they do know what
math is about, and are apparently under the

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:37.949
gross misconception that mathematics is somehow
useful to society.

00:06:37.949 --> 00:06:44.050
I do quite relate to this next part where
he says that kids don't want something that

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.539
is relevant to their daily lives.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:52.229
You think something practical, like compound
interest is going to get them excited?

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:53.229
People enjoy fantasy.

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:59.199
And that is just what mathematics can provide,
a relief from daily life.

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:04.090
Maths and doesn't need to be made interesting
by word problems.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:08.569
It's already full of some great human stories.

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:15.039
What other subject shuns its primary sources,
beautiful works of art, by some of the most

00:07:15.039 --> 00:07:18.469
creative minds in history.

00:07:18.469 --> 00:07:22.259
Math class lacks problems.

00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:26.849
Genuine honest to goodness, natural human
questions.

00:07:26.849 --> 00:07:29.210
How long is the diagonal of a cube?

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:32.900
Do prime numbers keep going on forever?

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.159
Is infinity a number?

00:07:35.159 --> 00:07:38.759
How many ways can I symmetrically tile a surface?

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:45.050
The history of mathematics is the history
of mankind's engagement with questions like

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:51.919
these, not a mindless regurgitation of formulas
and algorithms.

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:57.710
A good question does not just sit there in
isolation, but serves as a springboard to

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.619
other interesting questions.

00:07:59.619 --> 00:08:06.449
If a triangle takes up half its box, then
what about a pyramid inside of a three dimensional

00:08:06.449 --> 00:08:07.509
box?

00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:12.800
There is a place for teaching technique when
the student gets to a spot where they need

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.169
it to overcome a frustration in their own
problem.

00:08:17.169 --> 00:08:23.360
The trouble is that math, like painting or
poetry is hard creative work.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.129
And that makes it difficult to teach.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:32.080
As a more serious example, let's take the
case of a triangle inside a semicircle.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:38.090
Now, the beautiful truth about this problem
is that no matter where on the circle, you

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:40.350
place the tip of the triangle.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.380
It always forms a nice right angle.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.180
It's not at all clear that this should be
true.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:50.120
It even seems unlikely.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.160
Shouldn't the angle change if I move the tip?

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.840
And what we have here is a fantastic math
problem.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.030
Is it true?

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.920
And if so, why is it true?

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.430
But students would be treated to this theorem.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:13.280
Let the triangle ABC be inscribed in a semicircle
with diameter AC, then the angle ABC is a

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:14.940
right angle.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:22.450
You can compare this unappetizing proof with
one devised by one of Paul's seventh graders.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:29.620
Take the triangle and rotate it around, so
it makes a four sided box inside the circle.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:35.760
Since the triangle got turned completely around,
the sides of the box must be parallel.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.430
So it makes a parallelogram.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:45.650
But it can't be a slanted box, because both
of its diagonals are diameters of the circle.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:50.590
So they are equal, which means it must be
an actual rectangle.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:56.570
And that's why the corner is always a right
angle.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:00.000
A good teacher can guide the discussion and
the flow of

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.580
problems so as to allow the students to discover
and invent mathematics for themselves.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:14.130
The essay finishes by saying that it's ironic
that people dismiss mathematics as the antithesis

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.130
of creativity.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:22.450
They are missing out on an art form older
than any book, more profound than any poem,

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.890
and more abstract than any abstract.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:31.750
Now, this essay is a lament not really a guide
on how to fix things.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:38.320
It's one person's opinion on what's wrong
with math education, and it was written in

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:39.320
2002.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:45.140
So maybe some things have changed, but maybe
a lot of things are still the same today.

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:50.270
Some of his ideas made me feel inadequate
to ever call myself a teacher.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:55.620
But I hope that keeping these ideas in mind
would help all of us to become better teachers

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:57.420
and students.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:03.450
This video was brought to you by my Patreon
supporters, so a big thank you to them.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:09.060
And a special shout out to today's Patron
cat of the day Peach.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:13.139
Thanks so much for watching to the end of
the video, and I hope to see you next time.

